╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ reduced_params_shared_entanglement                                                        │
│ patch_description        │ The current program has 78 parameters (vs. 60 and 66 in the prior programs) but shows     │
│                          │ worse validation accuracy (0.55 vs. 0.65 and 0.60). The key issue is that the hub         │
│                          │ entanglement layer uses 4 independent parameters (crx_hub_edge_0, cry_hub_edge_0,         │
│                          │ crx_hub_edge_1, cry_hub_edge_1), which are introduced per ansatz block, leading to        │
│                          │ excessive parameterization without corresponding performance gains.                       │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ **Analysis of prior successful candidates:**                                              │
│                          │ - First program (score 0.57): Uses 60 params with shared CRZ hub gates (all 4             │
│                          │ center-to-edge gates share one parameter)                                                 │
│                          │ - Second program (score 0.60): Uses 66 params with shared CRY hub gates (all 4            │
│                          │ center-to-edge gates share one parameter)                                                 │
│                          │ - Current program (score 0.55): Uses 78 params with per-edge hub parameters (mixed        │
│                          │ CRX/CRY with 4 different params)                                                          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The data suggests that **fewer, more aggressively shared parameters** perform better. The │
│                          │ current mixed CRX/CRY approach with per-edge parameters creates too many degrees of       │
│                          │ freedom without corresponding expressiveness gains for the tic-tac-toe problem.           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ **Proposed improvement:**                                                                 │
│                          │ 1. Reduce hub entanglement to use a single shared parameter across all 4 center-to-edge   │
│                          │ gates                                                                                     │
│                          │ 2. Use CRZ gates (which worked well in the first prior program) rather than mixed CRX/CRY │
│                          │ 3. This reduces parameters per block from 11 to 9 (33 total vs. 78), improving parameter  │
│                          │ efficiency                                                                                │
│                          │ 4. Keep the ring entanglement as fixed CZ gates (not parametrized), which reduces circuit │
│                          │ depth while maintaining connectivity                                                      │
│                          │ 5. This matches the seed architecture more closely, which had proven effectiveness        │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Expected outcome: Reduce generalization gap, improve validation accuracy by ~0.05-0.10,   │
│                          │ reduce train-test gap, and increase parameter efficiency while maintaining or improving   │
│                          │ overall accuracy.                                                                         │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
