│ patch_type               │ cross                                                                                     │
│ patch_name               │ ttt_mixed_ring_crx_cry                                                                    │
│ patch_description        │ This crossover-driven proposal integrates the best-performing elements from the two       │
│                          │ reference implementations:                                                                │
│                          │ - Symmetry-aware, per-group pre- and post- RZ gates (corners/edges/center) to exploit     │
│                          │ Tic-Tac-Toe geometry.                                                                     │
│                          │ - Independent, group-shared RY rotations (corners/edges/center) for expressive yet        │
│                          │ parameter-efficient encoding.                                                             │
│                          │ - Ring entanglement on the outer border using CRX with a single shared parameter to       │
│                          │ promote coherent perimeter correlations.                                                  │
│                          │ - Hub entanglement between the center and edges using CRY with per-edge unique parameters │
│                          │ to increase flexibility without exploding parameter count.                                │
│                          │ - Post-entanglement RZ rotations still shared per group.                                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This design aims to improve validation/test performance by increasing expressivity where  │
│                          │ it matters (ring and hub interactions) while keeping parameter count manageable through   │
│                          │ targeted sharing. The rest of the training loop, data construction, feature map, and      │
│                          │ measurements remain unchanged to ensure fair comparisons. The approach preserves the same │
│                          │ inputs/outputs and hardware-constrained connectivity, while potentially narrowing the     │
│                          │ train/test gap observed in prior runs.                                                    │
