╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ cross                                                                                     │
│ patch_name               │ hybrid_crx_crz_hub_entanglement                                                           │
│ patch_description        │ This crossover combines the best aspects of three prior implementations:                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **From Current Program (score 0.60, best validation 0.65)**: Uses parametrized CRX     │
│                          │ gates on the ring with a single shared parameter, which provides efficient entanglement.  │
│                          │ This achieves the highest validation accuracy (0.65) among all candidates.                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. **From Second Prior (score 0.57, 60 params)**: Uses CRZ gates for hub entanglement     │
│                          │ with a single shared parameter (`crz_hub_edge`), which is more parameter-efficient than   │
│                          │ per-edge parameters.                                                                      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 3. **From First Prior (score 0.55, 78 params)**: Provides the foundational hierarchical   │
│                          │ structure with symmetry-based parameter sharing for single-qubit rotations.               │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ **Key Crossover Decision**:                                                               │
│                          │ - Keep the **CRX ring with shared parameter** from the current program (which achieved    │
│                          │ 0.65 validation accuracy)                                                                 │
│                          │ - Replace the CRY hub entanglement with **CRZ hub entanglement with shared parameter**    │
│                          │ from the second prior (reducing parameters from 66 to 60 while maintaining                │
│                          │ expressiveness)                                                                           │
│                          │ - Retain the solid single-qubit layer structure with symmetry-based parameter sharing     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This hybrid approach aims to:                                                             │
│                          │ - Maximize validation accuracy (keeping the successful CRX ring)                          │
│                          │ - Reduce parameter count from 66 to 60 (improving parameter efficiency)                   │
│                          │ - Maintain parameter efficiency by using only 1 hub parameter instead of mixing multiple  │
│                          │ gates                                                                                     │
│                          │ - Preserve the hierarchical structure that has proven effective                           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Expected outcome: Higher validation accuracy (≥0.65) with better parameter efficiency due │
│                          │ to reduced parameter count, potentially improving the combined score above 0.60.          │
